{
  "format": "sfxgen/1",
  "name": "Footsteps: snow",
  "description": "A loop of eight steps in boots through deep fresh snow on a still winter day, at a slow, careful walk. No impact anywhere: each step is a soft press, the weight sinking into the pack in a few pushes as the heel, the ball and the toe go down, and no two alike: one stops short, the next settles on and on, thick or sparse. A muffled low whumpf carries most of it, with a fine, quiet, grainy crunch of compacting snow over it that swells with each push; after it a few loose crystals trickle back into the print. Every step is a different handful of grains, left and right feet weigh differently, each is a little bigger or smaller, earlier or later; on a few steps the boot breaks through a thin frozen crust with faint brittle snaps, twice it brushes through the surface on its way forward, and a little powder comes off the toe. Snow swallows sound, so it is close and dead with no echo. Speed takes it from a slow trudge to a run: quicker, shorter, harder presses and snow kicked up behind nearly every stride. The loop is exactly eight step intervals long and wraps, so it repeats without a seam.",
